As illustrated in @fig-cross-validation, the validation observations change in every iteration. This produces a more stable estimate of generalization performance than a single train–validation split.

### Manual implementation

The following implementation exposes the internal logic of stratified cross-validation. Stratification preserves approximately the same class proportions in every fold.

::: {#manual-cross-validation .cell execution_count=11}
``` {.python .cell-code code-fold="true"}
from sklearn.base import clone
from sklearn.model_selection import StratifiedKFold

skfolds = StratifiedKFold(
    n_splits=3,
    shuffle=True,
    random_state=42
)

for fold, (train_index, valid_index) in enumerate(
    skfolds.split(X_train, y_train_5),
    start=1
):
    clone_clf = clone(sgd_clf)

    X_train_fold = X_train[train_index]
    y_train_fold = y_train_5[train_index]
    X_valid_fold = X_train[valid_index]
    y_valid_fold = y_train_5[valid_index]

    clone_clf.fit(X_train_fold, y_train_fold)
    y_valid_pred = clone_clf.predict(X_valid_fold)

    accuracy = (y_valid_pred == y_valid_fold).mean()
    print(f"Fold {fold} accuracy: {accuracy:.4f}")
```

