## Why accuracy is not enough

The accuracy scores may appear impressive because they exceed 90%. However, only about 10% of the images are 5s. A classifier that always predicts *not 5* would therefore be correct roughly 90% of the time without identifying a single positive observation.

The following baseline classifier always predicts the majority class.

::: {#never-five-classifier .cell execution_count=13}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from sklearn.base import BaseEstimator

class Never5Classifier(BaseEstimator):
    """Classifier that always predicts the negative class."""

    def fit(self, X, y=None):
        return self

    def predict(self, X):
        return np.zeros(shape=(len(X),), dtype=bool)
```
:::


::: {#evaluate-never-five .cell execution_count=14}
``` {.python .cell-code}
never_5_clf = Never5Classifier()

baseline_scores = cross_val_score(
    never_5_clf,
    X_train,
    y_train_5,
    cv=3,
    scoring="accuracy"
)

print("Baseline accuracies:", baseline_scores)
print("Mean baseline accuracy:", baseline_scores.mean())
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
Baseline accuracies: [0.91125 0.90855 0.90915]
Mean baseline accuracy: 0.90965
```
:::
:::


::: {.callout-warning title="Accuracy and class imbalance"}
A high accuracy does not necessarily imply that a classifier recognizes the class of interest. When one class is much more frequent than another, accuracy may be dominated by the majority class.

For imbalanced datasets, the confusion matrix, precision, recall, the F1-score, and precision–recall curves are generally more informative.
:::

# Confusion matrix

## Cross-validated predictions

A confusion matrix requires one prediction for every observation. These predictions should be generated without training the model on the same observations it is predicting.

`cross_val_predict()` satisfies this requirement. Each observation receives a prediction from a model trained on the other folds.

::: {#cross-validated-predictions .cell execution_count=15}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_predict

y_train_pred = cross_val_predict(
    sgd_clf,
    X_train,
    y_train_5,
    cv=3
)
```
:::


## Structure of the matrix

For binary classification, the confusion matrix contains four quantities:

```{dot}
//| label: fig-confusion-matrix
//| fig-cap: "Structure of a binary confusion matrix."
//| fig-align: center

digraph confusion_matrix {
    graph [
        bgcolor="transparent",
        pad=0.2,
        nodesep=0.05,
        ranksep=0.05
    ];

    node [
        shape=box,
        style="filled",
        fontname="Arial",
        fontsize=11,
        width=2.1,
        height=0.65
    ];

    title1 [label="", style=invis, width=1.8];
    predneg [label="Predicted negative", fillcolor="#EAF2F8"];
    predpos [label="Predicted positive", fillcolor="#EAF2F8"];

    actualneg [label="Actual negative", fillcolor="#EAF2F8"];
    tn [label="True negative (TN)", fillcolor="#E8F5E9"];
    fp [label="False positive (FP)", fillcolor="#FDEDEC"];

    actualpos [label="Actual positive", fillcolor="#EAF2F8"];
    fn [label="False negative (FN)", fillcolor="#FDEDEC"];
    tp [label="True positive (TP)", fillcolor="#E8F5E9"];

    {rank=same; title1; predneg; predpos;}
    {rank=same; actualneg; tn; fp;}
    {rank=same; actualpos; fn; tp;}

    title1 -> predneg -> predpos [style=invis];
    actualneg -> tn -> fp [style=invis];
    actualpos -> fn -> tp [style=invis];
    title1 -> actualneg -> actualpos [style=invis];
    predneg -> tn -> fn [style=invis];
    predpos -> fp -> tp [style=invis];
}
```

As shown in @fig-confusion-matrix:

- **True negatives (TN):** non-5 images correctly classified as non-5;
- **False positives (FP):** non-5 images incorrectly classified as 5;
- **False negatives (FN):** images of 5 incorrectly classified as non-5;
- **True positives (TP):** images of 5 correctly classified as 5.

The matrix can be computed as follows:

::: {#cell-binary-confusion-matrix .cell execution_count=16}
``` {.python .cell-code}
