# Multilabel classification

In multiclass classification, each observation belongs to exactly one class. In **multilabel classification**, an observation may belong to several classes simultaneously.

For illustration, each digit receives two labels:

1. whether it is large, meaning 7, 8, or 9;
2. whether it is odd.

::: {#cell-create-multilabel-target .cell execution_count=48}
``` {.python .cell-code}
y_train_large = y_train >= 7
y_train_odd = y_train % 2 == 1

y_multilabel = np.c_[
    y_train_large,
    y_train_odd
]

y_multilabel[:10]
```

::: {#create-multilabel-target .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=48}
```
array([[False,  True],
       [False, False],
       [False, False],
       [False,  True],
       [ True,  True],
       [False, False],
       [False,  True],
       [False,  True],
       [False,  True],
       [False, False]])
```
:::
:::


A K-nearest-neighbors classifier supports multilabel targets.

::: {#train-multilabel-knn .cell execution_count=49}
``` {.python .cell-code code-fold="true"}
from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier

knn_clf = KNeighborsClassifier()
knn_clf.fit(X_train, y_multilabel)

print(
    "Prediction [large, odd]:",
    knn_clf.predict([some_digit])[0]
)
```

