```
Prediction [large, odd]: [False  True]
```
:::
:::


Multilabel models can be evaluated with class-specific metrics or averaged scores. The appropriate choice depends on whether labels are equally important and whether label frequencies differ substantially.

# Multioutput classification

**Multioutput classification** generalizes multilabel classification by allowing each output to take more than two possible values.

A classic example is image denoising. The model receives a noisy image and predicts the intensity of every output pixel. Each of the 784 pixels is treated as a separate multiclass output.

::: {#create-noisy-mnist .cell execution_count=50}
``` {.python .cell-code code-fold="true"}
rng = np.random.default_rng(42)

noise_train = rng.integers(
    0,
    100,
    size=X_train.shape,
    dtype=np.uint8
)

noise_test = rng.integers(
    0,
    100,
    size=X_test.shape,
    dtype=np.uint8
)

X_train_noisy = np.clip(
    X_train + noise_train,
    0,
    255
)

X_test_noisy = np.clip(
    X_test + noise_test,
    0,
    255
)

y_train_clean = X_train
y_test_clean = X_test
```
:::


